[Music]  good evening i'm trey gowdy welcome to  sunday night in america  to those of you who joined us last week  thank you  you made our first show better than we  ever could have imagined or hoped for  to those of you joining us for the first  time tonight welcome  and thank you last sunday i posed a  question to tim scott that's worth  posing to ourselves  the question was simply this what is the  state of our union  what is the state of our country what  gives you  reason for optimism and what gives you  calls for concern  and we'll get to the good news before we  say goodbye tonight  for now let's take the more difficult  question what  concerns you about the state of our  union  and the quietness of wherever you are as  you reflect on the state of our country  what gives you calls for concern about  where we are  as a people one of the things senator  scott and i discussed was the need for  proportion for balance where there is or  has been pain  progress and promise all three warrant  attention  but in what order in what proportion  and no doubt each of us has our own  personal battles to wage an  illness a sick family member financial  concerns the loss of a job  those concerns are real and they  rightfully occupy our time  and attention but i'm thinking more  generally  as a people as a country what gives  us reason for pause and what concerns me  is precisely what has concerned me for  several years now  the absence of a societal referee the  absence  of a neutral detached societal  umpire we don't have to agree with every  decision that umpire or referee makes  but there's a difference between not  liking a decision  and not trusting the decision maker  the absence of a societal referee  coupled  with the absence of fairness as a virtue  is what concerns me when the reality or  perception of  unfairness seeps in the impact is  devastating  once lost trust is very hard to build  back again  i watch a lot of college sports my wife  might tell you  that's all he does is watch college  sports and this is a busy time for those  of us who love  college sports ncaa golf for men and  women  ncaa softball and baseball track and  field  gymnastics there is something pure  about college sports i had an ncaa  baseball game on in my home office  watching my beloved south carolina  gamecocks  and of course there's an umpire in  baseball and that umpire calls balls and  strikes and we expect the strike zone to  be the same for everyone  that's what fairness is the same rules  apply equally to everyone  a south carolina batter was called out  on a check swing  and it was not a good call so i began to  feel  this sense of being treated unfairly  building within me  and then a south carolina pitcher has a  pitch called  a ball that looked like a strike to me  and everyone else on the planet  and the sense of being treated unfairly  began to build  even more apparently it built into  something vocal  because my wife came into the office and  said please tell me you're not yelling  at the television  please tell me you are not yelling about  a college baseball game  a hundred miles away the umpire  cannot hear you she had a good point  so i walked outside in the backyard and  yelled even louder thinking well maybe  he can hear that  a grown man should not be yelling at a  tv  set over a college baseball game  probably but the perception of  unfairness  impacts us it changes us it makes  us do things we would not ordinarily do  being falsely accused or convicted is  terrible  so is believing all the calls go against  you  and the deck is stacked  i'm sure the umpire was doing the best  he could i'm sure there were some calls  some close calls that went our way over  the weekend but in that moment  when i perceived unfairness the impact  was real  and it was negative that's what the  perception  of unfairness can do to us what about  when the perception  actually becomes reality what if the  societal referee  really is biased what if the national  umpire really is calling it differently  for this team than for the other team  if you were to ask half our country  what's the biggest issue we're facing  as a country they might say unfairness  and economic opportunity  or unfairness in our justice system i  mean we hear it a lot  we hear about rigged systems and  everybody doesn't play by the same rules  meanwhile the other half of the country  also believes the system is rigged  it's just a different system rigged by  different people  the other half believes it's the media  it's higher education  it's politics that is stacked or rigged  or biased against one orthodoxy or  ideology the symptoms are different but  the underlying pathology  is the same and the underlying pathology  is a belief that unfairness is pervasive  we can handle mistakes we should not  accept a lack of fairness from any  institution  the institution which is supposed to be  that neutral  detached referee is the media supposed  to be  but at least half the country believes  the referee  is really pulling for the other side the  other institution we frequently hear  cited as a societal referee is the  justice system  and that too is under assault and for  reasons that are not hard to see in some  instances  so we have a choice as a country we can  pick a sport  we can be like golf where the players  call the penalties on themselves  it's a game rooted in honor and  integrity where people  actually police themselves we can be  like baseball  where there's an umpire but we must  insist and require that that umpire be  fair  or we can keep trending toward  professional wrestling  where there are no rules there's no  fairness and the end justifies the means  the outcome is fixed and is all just for  entertainment  i choose golf calling penalties on  ourselves  i'll settle for baseball with a fair  umpire who treats both sides the same  what i will not accept and what i fear  is a political system degrading itself  into professional wrestling that's what  i fear  what about you hey sean hannity here hey  click here to subscribe to fox news  youtube page  and catch our hottest interviews and  most compelling analysis  you will not get it anywhere else

